= Network interface controller — Simple =
<onlyinclude>A '''network interface controller''' (NIC), often called a network adapter, is the hardware that lets a computer send and receive data over a network. An Ethernet NIC turns computer data into frames for a wired connection and delivers received frames to the operating system. It may be built into a motherboard or installed as an expansion card.</onlyinclude>

== What it does ==
When a computer sends data, its operating system asks a driver to hand packets to the NIC. The NIC sends them over the connection. For incoming data, the NIC receives frames and makes them available to the computer. The driver coordinates this process.

== What it is not ==
A NIC is not a router, an entire computer network, or the software driver that controls it. A fast-rated connection does not mean every application reaches that speed: protocols, congestion, packet sizes, and host processing matter.

